## 0. Why this phase exists — the exact bugs, with receipts

This isn't a hypothetical cleanup. I read `lib/workspace-store.ts`, `lib/builder-store.ts`, `mock/data.ts`, and the publish/version-restore pages line by line. Here is what is **actually broken today**, so you understand exactly what you're fixing and why each fix matters:

1. **`CanvasNote[]` and `CanvasFrame[]` (sticky notes and section frames) are optional fields (`notes?`, `frames?`) only on the `Bot` type.** They do not exist at all on `Preset`, `MyPreset`/`StoredPreset`, or `PublishedPreset`. Every other entity that carries a graph silently has no way to carry notes or frames.

2. **`forkPreset(preset: Preset)` in `lib/workspace-store.ts`** copies `preset.nodes` and `preset.edges` into a new bot via `createBot(...)`, but `createBot`'s input type doesn't even accept `notes`/`frames` as parameters, and `Preset` doesn't have them to pass in the first place. **Result: forking a marketplace preset today always produces a bot with zero sticky notes and zero section frames**, even if the original creator carefully labeled and grouped their graph.

3. **`savePreset` builds a `StoredPreset` object that does store `nodes`/`edges`** (because `StoredPreset = MyPreset & { nodes?: BotNode[]; edges?: BotEdge[] }`), **but never captures `notes`/`frames`** — the type doesn't declare them and the function signature doesn't accept them. Saving your own bot as a reusable preset silently drops every sticky note and every labeled section.

4. **`duplicatePreset(id)`** spreads the existing `StoredPreset` object, so it only clones whatever that object already has — which per #3 is never notes/frames.

5. **The marketplace publish flow is the worst of these.** In `app/app/marketplace/publish/[botId]/page.tsx`, `handlePublish()` builds a `publishedItem` object with fields `{ id, name, clones, revenue, rating, reviews, publishedAt, price }` — **it never reads `bot.nodes`, `bot.edges`, `bot.notes`, or `bot.frames` at all.** Publishing a bot to the marketplace today captures precisely zero graph content. The public `/marketplace/:presetId` pages that show a browsable, forkable graph must be reading from a separate hardcoded `Preset[]` fixture that has no real connection to what a real user published. This is the single biggest gap behind what you're worried about: **today, there is no code path where "clone another trader's published bot" actually reproduces their real graph** — it can't, because publishing never captured it.

6. **`snapshotVersion(id, note)`** deep-clones `nodes` and `edges` into the new `BotVersion`, but not `notes`/`frames`. Version history has never preserved canvas annotations.

7. **The version-restore page** (`app/app/builder/[botId]/version/[versionId]/page.tsx`) compounds #6: its `load()` preview call passes `bot.notes, bot.frames` — the **current, present-day** notes/frames — for what's supposed to be a preview of an **old** version. And `handleRestore()` calls `saveGraph(bot.id, nodes, edges)` with no third argument, so `saveGraph`'s fallback (`annotations?.notes ?? b.notes`) just keeps whatever notes/frames currently exist. Net effect: restoring an old version has never restored that version's annotations, because they were never captured, and the "preview" of an old version has always silently shown current-day sticky notes instead of period-accurate ones.

8. **There is a real, already-encountered position-format inconsistency.** `lib/builder-store.ts` has a `migratePositions()` function that detects "legacy" fixtures where `y` was stored as a lane *index* (0–9) instead of a pixel offset, and promotes them. This proves the shape has already drifted once with no formal versioning to catch it. It will drift again with no schema version to gate migrations.

Every fix below exists to close one of these eight numbered gaps. Keep the numbers in mind as you implement — you'll reference them in your own commit/session notes.

