## 6. `lib/builder-store.ts` changes

- Change `Snapshot` (used for undo/redo history, clipboard, and copy/paste) to just be `BotGraph` — it already has the same four fields plus now `schemaVersion`, so this is a rename/type-alias, not a new shape: `type Snapshot = BotGraph`.
- `load(botId, nodes, edges, notes?, frames?)` — keep this signature as-is (it's called from multiple places with a graph broken into pieces, which is fine for a function signature — the point of this phase is the *stored* shape, not every function argument list). Internally, have it construct one `BotGraph` object immediately and work from that.
- Fix `migratePositions()` to be schema-version-aware instead of shape-sniffing:

```ts
/**
 * Migrates a graph to the current schema version. Currently handles the
 * single known legacy case (schemaVersion missing/1: y stored as a lane
 * index 0–9 instead of a pixel offset). Extend this function, not the
 * shape-sniffing heuristic, the next time the schema changes.
 */
function migrateGraph(graph: BotGraph): BotGraph {
  if (graph.schemaVersion >= CURRENT_GRAPH_SCHEMA_VERSION) return graph
  const legacyPositions =
    graph.nodes.length > 0 && graph.nodes.every((n) => Number.isInteger(n.y) && n.y >= 0 && n.y < LAYERS.length)
  const nodes = legacyPositions ? graph.nodes.map((n) => ({ ...n, y: laneCenterY(n.y) })) : graph.nodes
  return { ...graph, nodes, notes: graph.notes ?? [], frames: graph.frames ?? [], schemaVersion: CURRENT_GRAPH_SCHEMA_VERSION }
}
```

Call `migrateGraph` once, at the single point where a graph enters the builder (`load()`), not scattered across multiple read sites. This is the actual fix for bug #8 — instead of every consumer independently guessing whether a shape looks legacy, there's one gate, and it's driven by an explicit version number that future changes will also bump.

